# Gentian OS — per-cluster runtime settings template
#
# Usage:
#   ./install.sh --prepare-deployment
#   edit clusters/<cluster>/kernel/cluster-settings.env
#   commit and push it
#   ./install.sh
#
# Unlike install.env.template / install.secrets.env.template (which live and
# stay in this repo), this file's destination is gentian-deployments — one
# real, checked-in copy per cluster at
# clusters/<cluster>/kernel/cluster-settings.env, which install.sh requires.
# --prepare-deployment writes it with the values that run resolved, and never
# overwrites an existing one, so the copy in the repo is the authority. This
# reference lists every option, including the ones it leaves out.
#
# Network mode, storage class and mail config do not follow from KERNEL_DOMAIN
# and the stage the way the claims do, so a human states them here.
#
# Not here: KERNEL_DOMAIN. It has exactly one authored copy — the cluster's
# Crossplane Claim (clusters/<cluster>/kernel/claims/cluster.yaml) — scaffolded
# on first bootstrap from what you type at the KERNEL_DOMAIN prompt (or the
# KERNEL_DOMAIN env var, if already set). See docs/deployment.md §1.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Identity / URL topology
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# multi   — each tenant gets its own subdomain (<tenant>.KERNEL_DOMAIN) and
#           Keycloak realm (the common case).
# single  — one tenant occupies the whole cluster at KERNEL_DOMAIN directly.
TENANCY_MODE=multi

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Exposure model
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# tunnel     (default) — cluster sits behind a reverse-proxy/tunnel (e.g.
#              Cloudflare Tunnel); DNS resolves to the tunnel endpoint.
#              NODE_IP is not required.
# static-ip  — node has a public/reachable static IP; DNS points directly at
#              NODE_IP, which becomes required below.
NETWORK_MODE=tunnel

# How the edge LoadBalancer claims NODE_IP. Every provider spells this
# differently, so pick the preset that matches the cluster:
#
#   (unset)    spec.loadBalancerIP only — works on MetalLB, OpenStack, GCP
#   metallb    + metallb.universe.tf/loadBalancerIPs (address must be in a pool)
#   openstack  + keep-floatingip, so the floating IP survives Service deletion
#   gcp        loadBalancerIP with a reserved regional static address
#   aws        NLBs IGNORE loadBalancerIP, so it is omitted; supply the address
#              via LB_ANNOTATIONS with an EIP allocation, e.g.
#              LB_ANNOTATIONS="service.beta.kubernetes.io/aws-load-balancer-eip-allocations=eipalloc-0abc"
#
# Not used when NETWORK_MODE=tunnel — the edge Service stays ClusterIP.
# LB_PROVIDER=openstack

# Extra annotations for the edge LoadBalancer Service, "key=value" pairs
# separated by commas. Applied on top of whatever LB_PROVIDER contributes, so
# any provider not covered by a preset works without a code change.
# LB_ANNOTATIONS="service.beta.kubernetes.io/aws-load-balancer-eip-allocations=eipalloc-0abc"

# Required only when NETWORK_MODE=static-ip. Auto-detected from the cluster
# when unset in tunnel mode.
# NODE_IP=203.0.113.10

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Edge routing
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# gateway is the only supported value (Envoy Gateway + Gateway API). Must
# match the operator's Helm routingMode value.
ROUTING_MODE=gateway

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Mail behavior
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# external (default) — relay invitation/reset mail through an external SMTP
#            provider (e.g. Gmail, SES). Needs EXTERNAL_SMTP_* below, plus
#            SMTP_RELAY_USERNAME/SMTP_RELAY_PASSWORD in install.secrets.env.
# kernel     — use in-cluster Postfix/Dovecot. Needs NETWORK_MODE=static-ip
#            (kernel mail needs a reachable SMTP ingress; incompatible with
#            NETWORK_MODE=tunnel).
MAIL_SERVICE_MODE=external

# Required when MAIL_SERVICE_MODE=external. No default — must be set.
# EXTERNAL_SMTP_HOST=smtp.gmail.com
# Optional — defaults shown.
# EXTERNAL_SMTP_PORT=587
# EXTERNAL_SMTP_SSL=false
# EXTERNAL_SMTP_STARTTLS=true

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Infrastructure charts
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The PostgreSQL, MariaDB, Redis and MinIO charts the platform installs for
# tenant data services come from a public URL by default, which needs no
# credential — so the installer does not ask for one.
#
# Set this to true when INFRA_CHART_REPO points at a registry that requires a
# login; the installer then asks for the username and password and stores them
# at gentian-os/kernel/storage/registry.
# INFRA_CHART_PRIVATE=false
#
# Where the charts come from. Unset means the published packages for the
# gentian-os branch this installer runs from.
# INFRA_CHART_REPO=oci://registry.internal/gentian-infra

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Credential strategy
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# derived (default) — every app/service secret is HKDF-derived from
#            MASTER_PASSWORD (install.secrets.env); reproducible, no OpenBao
#            writes needed to rotate a whole cluster from one master secret.
# random     — each secret is independently random, generated once and
#            stored in OpenBao. No cross-cluster reproducibility.
# SECRET_MODE=derived

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# App bootstrap endpoints (optional — sane per-stage defaults, override only
# if this cluster's infra tier deviates from the standard naming)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Defaults to http://minio-<stage>.gentian-infra-<stage>.svc.cluster.local:9000
# MINIO_ENDPOINT=http://minio-dev.gentian-infra-dev.svc.cluster.local:9000
# Defaults to <cnpg-cluster>-rw.platform-kernel.svc.cluster.local
# CNPG_HOST=postgres-rw.platform-kernel.svc.cluster.local

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Storage
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# StorageClass used by kernel components that request persistent volumes
# (OpenBao, OpenBao transit, and the infra charts).
#
# Leave unset to use the cluster's own default StorageClass (the one annotated
# storageclass.kubernetes.io/is-default-class). install.sh resolves this during
# pre-flight and fails fast if the name does not exist on the cluster, rather
# than letting a PVC sit Pending forever with "storageclass not found".
#
# Set it only when this cluster needs a class that is NOT its default — e.g. an
# NFS class for ReadWriteMany, or a retain-policy class when the default
# deletes. The resolved value is passed to the kernel Helm releases, so it does
# not need to be repeated in any values file.
# STORAGE_CLASS=nfs-csi

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LLM serving (only used when GPU_ACCELERATION=true — see install.env.template)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Which model(s) this cluster's GPU(s) serve, and how — depends on the
# specific card(s)/cluster, not on gentian-os. One gentian-os cluster can
# run several named vLLM instances at once (e.g. a small always-on chat
# model plus a larger on-demand one) — one shared LiteLLM proxy sits in
# front of however many exist (see docs/design/agentic-ai.md §10.2).
#
# VLLM_INSTANCES is a space-separated list of instance IDs — pick short,
# memorable ones. Each ID must be a valid identifier (letters/digits/
# underscore, starting with a letter or underscore): it's used both as a
# suffix on this file's per-instance VLLM_<ID>_* variable names below
# (uppercased) and, lowercased with underscores turned into hyphens, as
# the Kubernetes resource-name suffix (vllm-<id>-inference, etc.).
# VLLM_INSTANCES="qwen"

# Per-instance settings, one block per entry in VLLM_INSTANCES above.
# Defaults shown are what install.sh falls back to per-instance if unset;
# state them explicitly here once you've picked a model so the choice is
# reproducible instance data, not a code default.
# VLLM_QWEN_MODEL_ID=Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct
# VLLM_QWEN_GPU_MEMORY_UTILIZATION=0.85
# VLLM_QWEN_MAX_MODEL_LEN=8192
# HF model caches run 15-40GB per 7-8B model; bump for a larger one.
# VLLM_QWEN_MODEL_CACHE_SIZE=60Gi
# Pin to a specific released tag (or digest, vllm/vllm-openai@sha256:...) for
# reproducible, content-verified pulls instead of the mutable :latest.
# VLLM_QWEN_IMAGE_TAG=latest
# Unset (default) disables tool/function calling -- chat clients that send
# tool_choice="auto" (Open WebUI's native tool support, agentic apps, etc.)
# get a clear 400 from vLLM instead of a silent misparse. Model-family
# specific -- e.g. "hermes" for Qwen2/Qwen2.5/Hermes-family models,
# "mistral" for Mistral, "llama3_json" for Llama 3 -- check vLLM's
# tool-calling docs for whatever VLLM_<ID>_MODEL_ID is actually set to.
# VLLM_QWEN_TOOL_CALL_PARSER=hermes
#
# A second instance would add its own ID to VLLM_INSTANCES above (e.g.
# VLLM_INSTANCES="qwen llama") plus its own block, e.g.:
# VLLM_LLAMA_MODEL_ID=meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct
# VLLM_LLAMA_GPU_MEMORY_UTILIZATION=0.35
# ...
#
# Remember: --gpu-memory-utilization is a fraction of ONE physical card's
# VRAM. Every instance scheduled onto the same GPU (time-sliced compute,
# shared memory pool — see GPU_TIME_SLICE_REPLICAS below) draws from the
# same pool, so concurrent instances' utilization values need to sum to
# comfortably under 1.0, not each independently approach it. On a
# multi-GPU-node cluster the scheduler can also place different instances
# on different physical cards entirely — nodeSelector/resources in
# vllm-gpu.yaml.tmpl don't pin an instance to a specific node.
# How many virtual slices the GPU Operator carves each physical GPU into
# (time-slicing) — depends on the specific card(s) installed, not on
# gentian-os. Applies whenever a GPU is present, regardless of
# GPU_ACCELERATION (it configures the GPU Operator's device plugin, not
# vLLM itself).
# GPU_TIME_SLICE_REPLICAS=4

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tenant namespace resource defaults (optional — defaults shown below).
# Written to gentian-cluster-config; consumed by the tenant-default and
# app-default Crossplane compositions. Lower these on resource-constrained
# homelab clusters; raise or omit on production-class clusters.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TENANT_LIMITRANGE_DEFAULT_CPU=500m
# TENANT_LIMITRANGE_DEFAULT_MEMORY=512Mi
# TENANT_LIMITRANGE_DEFAULT_REQUEST_CPU=100m
# TENANT_LIMITRANGE_DEFAULT_REQUEST_MEMORY=128Mi
# TENANT_INITJOB_LIMIT_CPU=500m
# TENANT_INITJOB_LIMIT_MEMORY=512Mi
# TENANT_INITJOB_REQUEST_CPU=100m
# TENANT_INITJOB_REQUEST_MEMORY=128Mi
